                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The game explores the idea that human altruism is often merely disguised egoism. It depicts people who want to help the \"broken\" but actually cause them harm, serving only their own needs by feeding on the attention and acclaim that come with playing the hero. The narrator thrives on the opinions of others and craves the image of a savior, going so far as to lie to and abuse the very person he claims to be helping. This seemingly virtuous narrator guides us through the game, highlighting how wronged the other person is and emphasizing his own efforts to assist them. The twist reveals that the narrator is a product of a populist society the \"good-natured\" one while the person he tries to help is the \"broken\" one. The crux of the matter is that the broken, withdrawn individual despite needing human contact and suffering from isolation bothers and harms no one; meanwhile, the would-be helper turns out to be a manipulator and a source of genuine harm. It is a subversive lesson on how altruism and populism often serve as a cover for egoism fueled by the applause of others. Society tries to convince itself that the problem lies with those who have withdrawn from it, yet it attempts to prove this by speaking on behalf of others manipulating everything in the process.",
